d "q-334569" :question/problem "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-(Δx)^{5}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.7,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.7$ to $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.7,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.7) & =5(0.7)^{4}-(0.7)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.2401-1\\cdot 0.168\\,07 \\\\  & =1.200\\,5-0.168\\,07 \\\\  & =1.032\\,43 \\\\  & ≈1.032.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.2) & =5(0.2)^{4}-(0.2)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.001\\,6-1\\cdot 0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.008-0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.007\\,68 \\\\  & ≈0.008.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.7$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.7}{0.2}=3.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}=(3.5)^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $1.032\\,43$ to $0.007\\,68.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{1.032\\,43}{0.007\\,68}=134.430\\,99…≈134.431.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3.5)^{p} & ≈134.430\\,99… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{3.5}⁡(134.430\\,99…) \\\\ p & ≈3.912….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-(Δx)^{5}\\,⇒\\,e(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{4})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-334569/field-1" "q-334569/field-2" "q-334569/field-3" "q-334569/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48cd517d-be8a-5dc2-91af-fbd19231363c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334569"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334542/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "e62caed9-6645-5d59-932c-50f28b9970be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334542/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "66cb5a24-943d-556c-a533-94fb9aef2ede" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334542/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "200dde8a-5f17-56a5-8461-180348dae7ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334542/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "1918ea97-72ce-5484-8de7-abf8e7e4e6bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334542/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "a9c01cf5-0d6a-55d2-bdb8-1f0f018f867d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334542/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a4075b71-d2c9-5f16-b014-1b9c3f35515f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-334542/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-334542/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-334542/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-334542/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-334542/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-334542/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334542" :question/id #uuid "133c2975-c93c-564e-ad25-860da7e0d9e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334542" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.12,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.12)=0.310.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.06,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.06)=0.078.$\n\nUse this data to estimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.12$ to $0.06.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.12}{0.06}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={2}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.310$ to $0.078.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.310}{0.078}=3.974….\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p} & ≈3.974… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(3.974…) \\\\ p & ≈1.990….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the approximate order of the method is $2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334542/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48cd517d-be8a-5dc2-91af-fbd19231363c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334542"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334546/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "7e2b2e00-462f-5e81-9c7f-6cf9419818c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334546/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "e560707e-91fc-5f7c-a9dd-40639c55c9b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334546/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "7eef39f2-6a1f-54e9-b106-7a22dd086789" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334546/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "d4210ce1-16de-556e-b34a-2214fdca9d99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334546/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "22783af8-4a41-5a89-b104-4ac67b4fc43f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334546/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "78cd4ffd-117a-59ee-ba69-0a28c5130a06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-334546/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-334546/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-334546/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-334546/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-334546/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-334546/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334546" :question/id #uuid "319b9230-63e8-5408-87a0-7bc7fecf8b48" :question/math-academy-id "q-334546" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.18,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.18)=0.486.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.06,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.06)=0.006.$\n\nUse this data to estimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.18$ to $0.06.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.18}{0.06}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={3}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.486$ to $0.006.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.486}{0.006}=81.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p} & ≈81 \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(81) \\\\ p & ≈4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximate order of the method is $4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334546/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48cd517d-be8a-5dc2-91af-fbd19231363c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334546"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186221922 :question/answer-fields 17592186220473] {:db/id "q-333702/selection/ma-v1/227c50ad6f4a989769545e67ec228f03e263f549ba8b1cbea6e92f6bad616d7c/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "53dea72e-ba3e-5886-ae86-aef8d3a3ae9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333702/selection/ma-v1/227c50ad6f4a989769545e67ec228f03e263f549ba8b1cbea6e92f6bad616d7c/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "35c6ff5f-b960-523f-a39b-4f6104cd1021" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333702/selection/ma-v1/227c50ad6f4a989769545e67ec228f03e263f549ba8b1cbea6e92f6bad616d7c/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "a38b2ea7-24b2-5c0c-a0ba-40e6f76e09f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333702/selection/ma-v1/227c50ad6f4a989769545e67ec228f03e263f549ba8b1cbea6e92f6bad616d7c/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "d3eab7d5-bd8b-5c86-b790-356242997f94" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333702/selection/ma-v1/227c50ad6f4a989769545e67ec228f03e263f549ba8b1cbea6e92f6bad616d7c/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "4ae0eadc-d07c-5bc2-af9b-242397448209" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333702/selection/ma-v1/227c50ad6f4a989769545e67ec228f03e263f549ba8b1cbea6e92f6bad616d7c" :answer-field/id #uuid "fc8b53eb-b07f-5a25-a094-5bfe3aa79338" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-333702/selection/ma-v1/227c50ad6f4a989769545e67ec228f03e263f549ba8b1cbea6e92f6bad616d7c/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-333702/selection/ma-v1/227c50ad6f4a989769545e67ec228f03e263f549ba8b1cbea6e92f6bad616d7c/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-333702/selection/ma-v1/227c50ad6f4a989769545e67ec228f03e263f549ba8b1cbea6e92f6bad616d7c/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-333702/selection/ma-v1/227c50ad6f4a989769545e67ec228f03e263f549ba8b1cbea6e92f6bad616d7c/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-333702/selection/ma-v1/227c50ad6f4a989769545e67ec228f03e263f549ba8b1cbea6e92f6bad616d7c/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-333702/selection/ma-v1/227c50ad6f4a989769545e67ec228f03e263f549ba8b1cbea6e92f6bad616d7c/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-333702"] :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.08,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.08)=0.13.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.02,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.02)=0.002.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.08$ to $0.02.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.08}{0.02}=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={4}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.13$ to $0.002.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.13}{0.002}=65.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{p+1} & ≈65 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{4}⁡(65) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.011… \\\\ p & ≈2.011….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333702/selection/ma-v1/227c50ad6f4a989769545e67ec228f03e263f549ba8b1cbea6e92f6bad616d7c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-334644/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "46a926f8-6569-5cc1-b1a1-19c689764027" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334644/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "d96bb35f-8d80-5835-9a31-288874061b4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334644/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "d45f6218-9aa4-5971-abf4-29912c091340" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334644/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "1f40bd7d-b60e-56b4-a894-f35111596526" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334644/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "73d75e1a-c18d-5436-8d73-ebf35a228889" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334644/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "80e67794-f7e0-5374-a5a5-abf39cccee4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-334644/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-334644/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-334644/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-334644/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-334644/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-334644/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334644" :question/id #uuid "acf7fb13-fc73-55d5-a726-fef5e6c5dbfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-334644" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.25,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.25)=0.50.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.10,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.10)=0.01.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.25$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.25}{0.10}=2.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}=(2.5)^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.50$ to $0.01.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.50}{0.01}=50.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.5)^{p+1} & ≈50 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2.5}⁡(50) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.269… \\\\ p & ≈3.269….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334644/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334644"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334650/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "4abd6296-2907-5318-8ad0-4483f1d6c2fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334650/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "f6f30b62-dcc1-5673-937c-1ad6e26aea5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334650/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "ce6a78d0-2892-5742-950f-ca436afb7f32" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334650/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "dbb4c304-b27e-59a6-88cb-98118bebe98f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334650/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "4a9e2b2a-325f-514a-96d4-e9c59787ef53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334650/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6e076e5c-67dc-5d81-a19b-b322e047552c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-334650/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-334650/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-334650/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-334650/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-334650/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-334650/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334650" :question/id #uuid "be7eeba5-8d10-5737-b3ca-fb43e58066a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-334650" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.20,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.20)=0.78.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.08,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.08)=0.008.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.20$ to $0.08.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.20}{0.08}=2.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}=(2.5)^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.78$ to $0.008.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.78}{0.008}=97.5.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.5)^{p+1} & ≈97.5 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2.5}⁡(97.5) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.99… \\\\ p & ≈3.99….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334650/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334650"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334724/field-1/c01be69fe812f7cb27bf7043bbb0bc85bda5ba8878dfb5ae47287b7fc571ee81" :answer/id #uuid "fe285bce-f31a-525b-80d8-5fd395fdda40" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.550" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334724/field-1/9fada88b7612c02337ec829dd89f2c6a29c9c4398a8681f279b5635642815f62" :answer/id #uuid "9b82ee08-4936-573e-bc73-f7402c90f9e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.281" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334724/field-1/b34544a9837d181994c7a132937df1564b327d325fab23b2c613d8bcfe8cb599" :answer/id #uuid "cee7c395-d278-5aed-8186-75bc553abdf4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.375" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334724/field-1/02e0a36f18c94973e1e96e511c218ec290df6ccd6e36a09919563b5a9dbce536" :answer/id #uuid "ede15536-dad0-5146-a759-65fc29cf9c3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.016" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334724/field-1/be2996522c88880b85c94a2df96123acadb7ac9f8da34e41793102bb8efe9a83" :answer/id #uuid "e1c331c3-e683-5479-af13-507bd57b5f6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.075" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334724/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7219ad08-2b52-5866-9d87-e2f5696e00a7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-334724/field-1/9fada88b7612c02337ec829dd89f2c6a29c9c4398a8681f279b5635642815f62" :answer-field/choices ["q-334724/field-1/c01be69fe812f7cb27bf7043bbb0bc85bda5ba8878dfb5ae47287b7fc571ee81" "q-334724/field-1/9fada88b7612c02337ec829dd89f2c6a